Two changes, both to guards. One closes a hole, one removes a block. Both
are disclosed in full below because changing a guard quietly is worse than
the bug.

1. verify-repo-hygiene SAID "no transport artifacts" AND MEANT SOMETHING ELSE

It scanned readdirSync(ROOT) — the working directory, at the repository
root. That misses the case that actually happens, and it missed it in
production:

  · a patch is uploaded through the GitHub web UI, which commits it to the
    branch being viewed and does not consult .gitignore, so the `*.patch`
    rule this repository already has does nothing;
  · the patch is applied locally and `git rm`'d;
  · the amend that would have carried the removal is refused by the
    pre-commit hook (see 2);
  · the working tree now holds no .patch file, so the guard printed
    "no transport artifacts" and exited 0;
  · HEAD still carried it, and the push shipped it inside ba429cf.

A guard whose success line is not true is worse than no guard, because the
sentence it prints is the one a person believes. It now also asks git what
is TRACKED, repo-wide, and fails with the exact two commands to fix it.

Proven both ways rather than asserted: a .patch on disk fails (it already
did), and a .patch that is tracked while absent from disk — the ghost case
that shipped — now fails too, where before it passed.

This is not a new rule. It is the rule the file already claimed to enforce.

2. THE PRE-COMMIT HOOK REFUSED EVERY COMMIT ON main

Its premise was that work reaches production through a pull request. It
does not, and never has here: production is `vercel --prod` from a working
tree, and scripts/ship.sh checks out main at step 1 and pushes main at step
7. The hook and the ship script contradicted each other outright, and the
only way through was ECOWOODS_ALLOW_MAIN_COMMIT=1 on every ship.

That is the worst state a guard can be in. A rule overridden every time is
a rule nobody reads, and the one time it is right it gets overridden by
reflex. It also cost an hour on this very series: `git commit --amend` was
refused mid-sequence, the staged deletion of a patch file was left behind
unnoticed, and that is how the artifact in (1) reached main.

F-152 is not being dismissed. What it actually was: a commit landed on
local main, `git push` reported success, and origin/main never moved. The
protection against that is not "never commit on main" — it is reading
origin/main back over the network after the push, which ship.sh step 7
already does and which is strictly better, because it catches the failure
on every branch rather than one.

So the block is now a notice. Nothing is refused. The old behaviour is one
`exit 1` away and the comment says where.

WIDENING, DISCLOSED. Change 2 removes a refusal and blocks nothing that was
previously allowed — no agent or person is newly obstructed. Change 1 makes
an existing check honest rather than adding a new one; the only commits it
newly refuses are commits that carry a transport artifact, which is the
thing this repository has been fighting since patch 53.

`bash scripts/ship.sh "subject"` now runs end to end on main with no
environment variable.

Verified: 65 of 66 guards (verify:css needs postcss from node_modules,
absent in this sandbox). parse-scan 606 files, zero diagnostics. Hook
syntax-checked with `bash -n` and exercised on both a feature branch and
main.

